{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"relational database relational database","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","fileName":"Relational Database.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Relational Database","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Functions Cardinality and Relational Data]] [[Cloud Data Storage Selection and Consistency]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Functions Cardinality and Relational Data","Cloud Data Storage Selection and Consistency","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"relational database relational database","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","fileName":"Relational Database.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Relational Database","heading":"Relational Database","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A relational database stores data in tables that are mathematical relations: sets of ordered tuples drawn from Cartesian products of data types. Rows are tuples or records, and named columns identify their components.\n\nSQL queries select rows by predicates, project chosen columns, and combine tables through joins that begin from a product and retain matching records. A database management system also supports insertion, deletion, updating, and query optimization.\n\nFor cloud application storage, relational databases add schema constraints, referential checks, and transaction isolation. These guarantees are valuable for connected invariants but require coordination that can make geographically distributed writes harder to scale.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Functions Cardinality and Relational Data","Cloud Data Storage Selection and Consistency","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"relational database relational database","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Relational Database.md","fileName":"Relational Database.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Relational Database","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]\n[[hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Functions Cardinality and Relational Data","Cloud Data Storage Selection and Consistency","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}